    // RAN: Review gate run 2026-11-11 for Sprint 5 (E4 e2eeRealServerE2e) on 4fdac66, three passes per D0172 by one independent reviewer agent. Pass 1 found six Medium and four Low findings on the spec. The dedup scenario did no second upload. The substitution was a client-side wrapper, not the server. The 403 scenario used a never-synced client and a test-built error. The corrupt-index scenario used two fresh clients, asserted no quarantine, and compared rows on hash and cid only. The grep missed the migrated vault's marker. Pass 2 named the rules of spec sections 4.3 to 4.7 that the scenarios exercise and the ones they leave to the unit suites. Pass 3 marked clauses 1 to 6 PASS-WITH-DEVIATION, clause 7 FAIL per D0112, and clause 8 FAIL at that commit with the evidence it needs. Every Medium is fixed in the closing commit. Dedup now pushes the same bytes under a second path on the same device and a third path on a cold device, with zero uploads. The real server serves one file's row under another path through a commit. The victim refuses it by the cid, with equal sizes on record. The pre-migration plaintext client meets the 403 with a populated base, byte-identical after. The same two clients survive the corrupt-index restart with no errors and unchanged file sets. The quarantine file is asserted, and rows compare on hash, cid, size and chunks. The migrated vault's marker is in the grep. The helpers subclass the shared transport, and the spec gates on the local binary. The reworked clause 5 found a real defect. The in-sync branch of reconcileOne stored the frame hash as the base identity, so an encrypted vault cascaded conflict copies on every round trip. Fixed at the one site, guarded by a lint assertion, tracked as issue017. Verdict PASS.
    part e4RealServerE2eReviewGateR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "b5bb0b08-fc08-4b60-a3db-254c394431f5"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::proposed; :>> judgedAgainst = "4fdac66"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-11-11"; :>> judgedBy = "ai"; }
