    // RAN: Retro 2026-11-11 for Sprint 5 (E4 e2eeRealServerE2e). What the panel-fixed DoD got right: the six server-side clauses named concrete observables. So the reviewer could show where a scenario asserted less than its clause. What the implementation changed: the real server now serves the substituted row, the same clients survive the rebuild, and dedup is a real second upload. The reworked clause 5 found issue017, a High defect that no unit test saw. An encrypted vault cascaded conflict copies because one in-sync site stored the frame hash as the base identity. The lesson is the same as Sprint 4, one level up. The unit suites pinned the pure rules and the plugin paths. Only a real round trip over a real server showed the base drift. Only the reviewer's push for the same clients across the restart made the test see it. The guard is a lint assertion over reconcile.ts and the convergence assertion of the spec. Avoidable issues: the first cut of the spec asserted weaker properties than its clauses, caught by the review before closeOut. No new process item: the review step did its job. Already tracked: issue014, issue015, issue016 and issue017, all under the tree of d0096. No new item: every follow-up is already tracked under d0096 as a task or an issue, so no backlog item is added. Wall time: about one sitting against the 8-point guideline of two to three days. The estimate was conservative.
    part e4RealServerE2eRetroGateR1 : TestResult { :>> id = "39e9b79f-4e80-43ce-b48d-b209cf7be11e"; :>> outcome = VerdictKind::proposed; :>> judgedAgainst = "3bd97c8"; :>> judgedAt = "2026-11-11"; :>> judgedBy = "ai"; }
